WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.450
- Allow me to put on my
shill hat for a moment.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.160
Everyone else is gonna tell you

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.820
what's wrong with Intel's Arc A700 GPUs.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.670
And, we're gonna need
to talk about that too

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.180
because it's a pretty sizable list.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.750
I mean, we've got the A770 losing

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.410
to a card from almost
10 years ago in CS:GO?

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.240
How embarrassing.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.460
But what's this?

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.300
It beats and NVIDIA's 3060 in Fortnite.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.070
It absolutely murders the
Radeon 6600 in Ray Trace games,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.417
and is available starting at
just around its $289 msrp.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.580
Something I will never
take for granted again.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.170
So it's with that in mind

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.353
that I want to explore
a different perspective.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.430
One where the benefits of competition

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.890
may outweigh the risk of buying

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.860
into a first generation product.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.370
That's right. It is
time to get blue pilled

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.070
with me folks. Or not that one.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.140
I meant like Intel Blue.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.250
Though I am pretty excited right now.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.710
Excited to tell you about our sponsor.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.040
- Ridge. Ridge Wallet has
redefined the traditional wallet

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.310
with its compact frame and
R-F-I-D blocking plates.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:21.690
Because the bulge in your pants,

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.310
shouldn't be from your wallet.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.410
Use offer code Linus to save 10%

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.676
and get free worldwide shipping.

00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:29.934
(machine beeping)

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:32.517
(upbeat music)

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.740
- Intel's A750 and A770
GPUs are designed for gaming

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.330
at either 1440p with medium
details or 1080p Ultra.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:48.660
And they've got NVIDIA'S RTX 3060 directly

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.860
in their crosshairs, or
at least now they do.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.990
Their transistor count
and power consumption

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.440
are both more comparable to the RTX 3070

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.560
which would indicate that
Intel fell well short

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.540
of their original performance targets,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.260
and is now trying to make
lemonade out of these lemons.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.330
But that's not our problem.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.690
And from a consumer
standpoint, they kind of did.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:16.500
Because at $289, the A750

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.500
may just be the most silicon
per dollar ever in a GPU.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.630
And there are situations
where it really shows.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.570
Check out this performance
in Direct X12 games.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.020
1080p High has NVIDIA's 3060 taking nearly

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:32.910
every game by a thin margin.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.066
But when the resolution gets cranked 1440p

00:02:35.066 --> 00:02:39.087
at medium details, it's
Intel racking up small wins

00:02:39.087 --> 00:02:41.310
nearly across the board.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.260
And this is against a card
that costs $50 to $100 more.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.740
Even looking closer, the Arc
cards continue to impress.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.330
5% and 1% lows are very
comparable to the RTX 3060

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.360
which means you won't
feel any extra hitching

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.790
or stuttering in heavy firefights.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:03.420
And while I was concerned
that Arc might fall flat

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.340
on its face in a Horizon system,

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.080
that wasn't the case at all.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.970
Oh, and as an extra bonus,

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:13.350
Arc murders the more
price comparable RTX 3050.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.883
Isn't competition great?

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.990
This is the point in the
video where Intel's employees

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.810
and shareholders might
wanna stop watching.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.310
Because we're about to look at some games

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.400
that aren't Direct X12 or Vulcan.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.930
In Shadow of the Tomb Raider,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.930
Arc's performance falls to
nearly half in DirectX 11. Ouch!

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.810
And CS:GO, a DirectX 9
title where Arc feels

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.500
like a totally different card
every time you start it up.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:44.670
With the A750 beating the A770 sometimes,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.480
1440p results being
higher than 1080p results

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:51.960
and, oh by the way, no matter
which card you're looking at

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.540
they get absolutely wrecked in performance

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.340
by the 3050 flagship
GPU from nine years ago.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.580
And probably a competitive Abacus user.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.700
I mean, what happened to
crush Intel's performance

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.590
in these older titles?

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.300
Well in a nutshell,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.300
Arc isn't actually running
DirectX 9 games in DirectX 9.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.158
Instead, they're using a DirectX 9

00:04:17.158 --> 00:04:20.010
to DirectX 12 translation layer.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.680
This works because you can turn basically

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.760
any DX9 command into a
string of DX12 commands

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.760
which is great for compatibility.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.760
But in the process you are
adding a buttload of CPU overhead

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.070
so much that any small background task

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.040
can significantly affect your frame rates.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.250
Now, because of the extremely limited time

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.870
that we were given to benchmark Arc

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:47.070
before the embargo lift,

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.250
we weren't able to test a
wide variety of older games.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.860
This is actually a strategy
that we've seen often

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.640
when a manufacturer doesn't
want you to look too closely.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.650
Fortunately, we have a solution to that.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:02.400
Tomorrow we are gonna be live
for hours taking requests

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.950
from our community and gaming side by side

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:08.040
on the Arc A770 and RTX 3060.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.680
So you can see how your
favorite games run,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.930
or if they run at all.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.480
Because nothing at this
point is a guarantee.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.390
Intel told us point blank that they knew

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.360
about the performance challenges
of Arc in older games.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.770
And they had a strategy

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.440
where they were gonna price
this generation of cards

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.920
according to Hollywood perform
in a worst case scenario

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.710
or in Tier 3 games.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.220
And I'm not gonna go as far
as to say that Intel lied

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.910
which would imply premeditated
intention to deceive.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.720
But what I do have to say is
that these CS:GO results show

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.930
that they did not follow through on this.

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:48.030
I mean, charitably, we could say

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:51.773
that the Arc gets half of the
performance of an RTX 3060

00:05:51.773 --> 00:05:54.720
though at 10 UDP it's more like a third.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.400
So even if we're looking
at a $400 RTX 3060,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.400
these A700 series GPUs
should be at most 200 bucks.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.680
But if we go back to that
dye size conversation,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.030
I mean remember this is a GPU
that is larger than a 3070

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.300
and built on TSMCs
cutting edge and six node

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.280
rather than an Intel's own fab.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.940
So they can't exactly
give themselves a friends

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.710
and neighbors discount on the fabbing.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.060
So if they went any lower,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.870
they'd likely be losing money
on every card they sold.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.940
And it gets even worse.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.880
Unfortunately for Intel, who's
trying to position themselves

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.510
as the scrappy underdog
undercutting videos

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.700
monopolistic pricing,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.310
we already have scrappy underdog at home.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:46.310
It's AMD and their Radeon
6600 goes for about 250 bucks

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.610
and is able to truly trade
blows with the RTX 3060

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:55.610
without the in specific
DirectX 12 titles fine print.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.340
And although AMDs software
hasn't always been fantastic

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.650
if our community feedback
has anything to go by,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.320
the last couple of years have
been a real turning point.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.800
And their RDNA 2 cards
have aged like fine wine,

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.800
getting better with
each new driver update.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.710
I would hope that the
same will happen for Arc.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.290
And we actually have
seen a lot of improvement

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.640
since its first outing.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.130
Arc was actually impressively stable

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.800
for our first gen product
throughout our testing.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.200
But when it comes to raw performance,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.510
there are clearly some problems here

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.090
that can't be fixed without new silicon.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.180
Like Intel's reliance on Resizable BAR.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.130
ReBAR, as we've started calling it,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.560
is a feature that allows for much larger,

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.110
more efficient data dumps between the GPU

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.550
and the system memory.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.400
It's only available on systems
from the last few years

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.160
but for most people that's
not really a huge deal.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.730
Because performance on AMD
and NVIDIA cards only improves

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:55.730
by anywhere from 1% to at most about 10%.

00:07:55.798 --> 00:07:57.390
For Intel, on the other hand,

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.540
ReBAR is basically a necessity.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.910
Performance can end up so bad without it

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.700
that Intel said in
their own press briefing

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.050
that if you don't have it
you might as well get a 3060.

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.810
With this in mind then, we
point blank asked Intel,

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:17.810
why the heck would anybody buy
an Arc GPU over the RX 6600?

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.290
To which they replied,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:24.270
that the RX 6600 doesn't have
feature parity with the A750.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:27.360
When compared to Radeon
6000, Intel claimed,

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.690
Arc has much better rate
tracing, AI acceleration,

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.480
and media encoders that make
it a much closer competitor

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.290
to the RTX 3060.

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:39.030
And okay, yeah. If you
care about those things,

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.670
they actually have a pretty good point.

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:42.900
In Ray Tracing titles

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.860
The A770 is a tiny bit
behind the RTX 3060.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.240
And in Shadow of the Tomb Raider,

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.170
Intel's Xe Super Sampling
or XeSS ended up performing

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:55.230
very similarly to NVIDIA's DLSS 2.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:58.770
So yes, Intel. You do
very much have a leg up

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:02.580
on AMD in 100% of the Strand
type games on the market

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.140
along with the other 20 games
that actually support XeSS.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.240
But it's clear you'll
need to pull some tricks

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:10.950
out of your sleeves to get game developers

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.440
to adopt XeSS more broadly.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:14.640
That sleeve trick, by the way,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.330
is easy on our Short Circuit
hoodie, ltt store.com.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:20.310
It's not just game super sampling either.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:22.830
If you care about AI
performance more generally,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.190
Arc is kind of excellent and here's why.

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.130
Traditionally, matrix
operations are done using a MAC,

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.650
or a multiply, accumulate, combine.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.050
Which is okay at traditional workloads,

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.150
but can be pretty slow for AI.

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.580
On integrated graphics,
Intel has a fancier way

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:42.630
to MAC called DP4A where
it takes a 32-bit register

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.090
and splits it into 8-bit chunks,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.250
allowing for four parallel operations

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:48.690
to be performed at once,

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:51.750
improving throughput by about four times.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:55.140
The full-fat Arc cards like the A750,

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.170
take that to the next level with XMX

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.290
which allows you to do
multiple multiply accumulates

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.450
in parallel, which can improve throughput

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:07.290
by up to 16 times in theory.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.760
And in practice where the
A770 absolutely slaughtered

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.280
the competition in Topaz Video Enhanced AI

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.920
without running into any of
the issues that we experienced

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.290
in our A370 M review.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:23.550
Intel Arc also supports NextGen
AV1 encoding and decoding,

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.980
which is particularly
beneficial for streamers

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.960
due to its much higher image quality

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.840
at low bit rates compared to H.264.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.430
We actually have a video coming up

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:35.370
that discusses using a low end Arc GPU

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.220
as kind of an encoding co-processor.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:42.058
If you don't wanna
spend $900 on an RTX 47,

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:43.650
excuse me, sorry.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.620
I mean 4080 12 gig.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:51.480
Unfortunately, in more traditional
H.264 and H.265 encoding,

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:55.110
the RTX 3060 is able to handily embarrass,

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:57.300
even the top tier A770.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:59.940
And the same goes for Blender rendering.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.980
Though as a consolation prize,

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.400
Intel was at least able to
beat the 3050 in Blender

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:08.790
while AMD continued to make
its usual excellent case

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:12.480
for never using AMD if
you're a Blender artist.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.600
Intel also made a good case
for not using Arc on Linux

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:16.980
for the foreseeable future.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:19.440
Sorry guys. I know that
you had high hopes,

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.340
but currently Arc only
supports Kernel Version 6.

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.500
And Intel has said that they do not plan

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.210
on supporting Version 5.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:30.210
Meaning that every stable version of Linux

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:31.980
is currently not supported.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:33.090
To put this in perspective

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.890
for the non Linux folks out there,

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:37.410
this is basically like
Intel launching a product

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.293
but only having drivers for Windows 12.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:43.500
Oh, one thing I haven't mentioned yet

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.390
is that there are both 16 gig

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.640
and 8 gig versions of the A770.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.590
But the reason I didn't mention that

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:53.190
is that Intel's only doing
a small run of 16 gig cards

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:57.120
and the A750 is the far more
compelling option anyway

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.260
at $289 for nearly the same performance.

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:02.910
Now, thank you for that.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:05.550
I'm going to do something
that might surprise you.

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:09.480
I'm gonna ask you, you fine
viewer of Linus Tech Tips.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:13.290
You savvy source of tech support
for your friends and family

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:17.340
to seriously consider
Arc for your next GPU.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.830
Because somebody needs to do it,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.120
and it can't be your friend

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.310
who's shopping for prebuilt right now.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:26.670
You do not wanna help them
troubleshoot random edge cases

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.270
on this thing, but somebody has to do it.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.790
Because the last few
years have made it clear

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.760
that NVIDIA has no qualms
about gouging their customers

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.330
when there's a shortage or no shortage.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:40.980
And the thing is that,

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:43.290
everyone says they want more competition.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:46.770
But then they go ahead and
just buy the NVIDIA GPU

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:49.980
and hey thanks AMD for
keeping Team Green Honest.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.310
Glad you exist, except when you
don't keep Team Green Honest

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:56.400
because you also like
to jack up your prices

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.080
and sell the crypto miners.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.300
So there it is.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:01.950
If you're the technical type

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:04.500
and don't mind rolling
the dice once in a while,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.290
I'm asking you to give Intel Arc a shot.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.360
You might not get as many FPS,

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.770
but I think it's safe to say

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:13.230
you'll have a more interesting
hardware experience

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:14.940
than a 3060 owner will.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.980
You can even over clock the thing

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.530
or you could just ignore my plea

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:21.660
and wait for AMD's RDNA three to drop.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:23.640
So you can pick up a GPU that was designed

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.310
to take on NVIDIA's 4000 series

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.373
instead of one that failed
to take on the 3070.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.370
